{
  "image_name": "1b2a2a7f732d401660d3bb7e75e464da.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different chromosome–microtubule attachment states produce different strengths of spindle checkpoint signaling and, in turn, different rates of Cyclin B destruction. At the top, several kinetochore configurations are compared, ranging from properly bi-oriented chromosomes to attached but tensionless, syntelic, single unattached, and multiple unattached states. These conditions are linked to progressively stronger checkpoint output: bi-oriented chromosomes show little or no checkpoint, whereas unattached kinetochores—especially multiple unattached ones—show the longest checkpoint duration, highest MAD2 accumulation, and the slowest Cyclin B degradation. Intermediate attachment errors produce intermediate responses.\n\nThe lower part presents a model for why checkpoint strength varies. The key idea is that signaling at KNL1 MELT motifs depends on the balance between MPS1 kinase and PP1 phosphatase. In a weak response, reduced MPS1 and increased PP1 allow only limited recruitment of BUB1–BUB3 and MAD1–MAD2, generating relatively little MCC and therefore weak inhibition of APC/C. In a strong response, higher MPS1 and lower PP1 drive recruitment to more MELT sites, producing more MCC complexes bound to CDC20 and causing strong APC/C inhibition. Overall, the image emphasizes a graded, not all-or-none, spindle checkpoint response.",
